{"text": "Creams (1992) 102: S121-S127\n\nCHROMOSOMA\n\nStructural and functional relationships of human DNA polymerases\n\nHuiling Hao, Yunquan Jiang, $.J. Zhang, Peng Zhang, Rong X. Zeng, and Marietta Y.W.T. Lee\n\nDeparment of Medicine and Biochemistry & Molecular Biology, University of Mam, Miami, FL 33101, USA\n\nReceived Sepembr 18, 1992\n\n\u2018Abstract. A continuing theme of our laboratory has been\nthe understanding of human DNA polymerases at the\nstructural level, We have purified DNA. polymerases\ndelta, epsilon and alpha from human placenta, Monoclo-\nnal antibodies to these polymerases were isolated and\nused as tools to study their immunochemical relation\nships. These studies have shown that while DNA\npolymerases deta, epsilon and alpha are discrete pro-\nteins, they must share common structural features by\nvirtue of the ability of several of our monoclonal ant-\nbodies to exhibit cross-reactivity. A second approach we\nhave taken is the molecular cloning of human DNA\npolymerase delta and epsilon. We have cloned the DNA\npolymerase delta cDNA, and this has allowed us to\ncompare its primary structure to those of human poly-\nmerase alpha and other members of this polymerase\nfamily. Multiple sequence alignments have revealed that\nhuman DNA polymerase dltais also closely related to\nthe herpes virus family of DNA polymerases. In situ by-\nbridization has shown thatthe human DNA polymerase\ndeka gene is localized to chromosome 19 q133-al34\nIn order to futher determine the functional regions of\nthe DNA polymerase & structure we are currently \u00a2x-\npressing human pol 8 in E.colfand baculovirus systems.\n\u2018Other work in our laboratory is directed toward examin-\ning the expression of DNA polymerase 8 during the cell\ncycle.\n\nIntroduction\n\n\u2018The major focus of our laboratory has been on the\nenzymology and structural and functional relationships of\n\nCorrespondence to: MY.W.T. Lee\n\n\u2018human DNA polymerases. Our approach to the study of\n[DNA polymerase 6 can be viewed from the perspective\n\u2018of several important isves. These were 1) the question of\nwhether DNA polymerase \u00a7 was a distinct entity from\nDNA polymerase o,, bearing in mind that its existence\nwas initially doubted and that for nearly a decade studies\nofthe enzyme were limited to the research groups atthe\nUniversity of Miami; 2) the investigation ofits potential\nrole in DNA replication and/or repair; 3) the relationships\nbetween the different forms of DNA polymerase 6. In\nthis papec we will review the progress we have made in\nthis direction and also present aspects of the current work\ninthe laboratory.\n\nMaterials and methods\n\n\u2018SDS polyserylamide gel clectophoresis was performed and stai-\n\u2018ped with silver as previously described (Lee et al, 1991)\n\u2018Theeffect of PCNA on processivity of DNA polymerase delta\n\u2018was analyzed by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis ofthe reae-\ntion producs as deseibed by Prelch tal, 1987\nWeulem Bloting was as described in Lee et al, 1989\nImmunclocaizatio of DNA polymerase epsllon was performed\n8 described in Lee et al, 1991\n\u2018Norther Boting was performed as deseribed in Yang etal,\n1992,\n\nResults and discussion\n\nIsolation of human DNA polymerases: the existence of\ntwo DNA polymerases with associated 3to 5\" activites\n\nWe originally undertook to isolate pol from human\nplacenta, and as a consequence isolated two DNA poly\nrmerases which had associated 3'to 5\" exonuclease activi-\n\n~~ 40037468",
